    "snippet": "---\ntitle: Living Donor information\nauthor: Author Stephen Emmerson\ndate: 2019-04-11\n---\nAbout kidney disease Treatment for kidney disease Living Donor information If you would like to discuss your kidney diagnosis with our trained members of staff, ring the free to call number 0800 169 0936. The NKF Helpline is available Monday to Thursday 08:30am - 5:00pm Friday 08.30am \u2013 12.30pm on 0800 169 09 36 or email [email protected] If you have a relative or friend with kidney failure, the treatment options are dialysis and, in suitable patients, transplantation. Kidneys for transplantation come either from people who have donated those organs after their death, or from living relatives, friends, spouses or partners. This section of the NKF website has been written to give information to those considering live kidney donation. More information is always available from the doctor at your renal unit, transplant unit or the transplant coordinator. There will be a lot of verbal information given to prospective donors, both before and during the assessment period. This information has been prepared to make sure that all prospective live donors and their families can have the opportunity to read about the risks, benefits, investigations, procedure and follow-up associated with donating a kidney. Everything covered here will be discussed on a personal level by the medical staff. This information is in no way a replacement for face-to-face meeting between the transplant team, the prospective donor; the recipient and their families. Why is living donation required? A successful renal transplant is the best treatment for many patients with end stage kidney failure, from the medical, psychological and social points of view. About 3,000 kidney transplants are performed in Britain annually and this is increasing year on year. In Britain, the majority of transplanted kidneys are from those who have been died (known as cadaveric donors). In fact, the long-term success rate is better after transplantation from living donors than from cadaveric donors. Since the make-up of the body within families can be similar; or more rarely - as in the case of identical twins - the same, the likelihood of the recipient's body rejecting the new kidney, is smaller. Therefore, there is a greater chance of a successful kidney transplant if the kidney is donated by a living person. Non-related living donors may also be considered, although it is less likely they will be as good a match. However; the advantage of live kidney donation is"
  }
